DPDK is a set of libraries and drivers for fast packet processing.
It supports many processor architectures and both FreeBSD and Linux.

The DPDK uses the Open Source BSD-3-Clause license for the core libraries
and drivers. The kernel components are GPL-2.0 licensed.

Please check the doc directory for release notes,
API documentation, and sample application information.

For questions and usage discussions, subscribe to: users@dpdk.org
Report bugs and issues to the development mailing list: dev@dpdk.org

System Setup (Raspberry Pi 5 + RP1 MACB)

This fork enables the RP1 MACB / Cadence GEM to run in DPDK via UIO.
You must set up hugepages, bind the NIC to UIO, and load the DMA sync helper.


0. Build DPDK (one-time per source update)
------------------------------------------
Install prerequisites (Debian/Raspberry Pi OS):
  sudo apt update
  sudo apt install -y build-essential meson ninja-build pkg-config \
                      libnuma-dev python3-pyelftools libpcap-dev libbpf-dev clang

Configure and build (includes testpmd and the matrix-rx example):
  meson setup build -Dexamples=matrix-rx
  ninja -C build
  sudo ninja -C build install
  sudo ldconfig

Reconfigure an existing build (if you already have ./build):
  meson configure build -Dexamples=matrix-rx
  ninja -C build

Clean build:
  rm -rf build && meson setup build -Dexamples=matrix-rx && ninja -C build

1. Enable Hugepages (one-time)
--------------------------------
echo 1024 | sudo tee /proc/sys/vm/nr_hugepages

Persist across boots:
echo "vm.nr_hugepages=1024" | sudo tee -a /etc/sysctl.conf
sudo sysctl -p

Check:
grep Huge /proc/meminfo


2. Mount Hugepages (each boot)
-------------------------------
sudo mkdir -p /dev/hugepages
sudo mount -t hugetlbfs nodev /dev/hugepages

Optional /etc/fstab entry:
nodev /dev/hugepages hugetlbfs defaults 0 0


3. Bind RP1 NIC to UIO (each boot)
-----------------------------------
This detaches eth0 from Linux. Use another interface or serial for SSH.

Load UIO:
sudo modprobe uio

Find RP1 MACB device:
ls /sys/bus/platform/devices | grep -i eth

Bind to UIO (replace NAME if different):
NAME=fe00a000.ethernet

echo "uio" | sudo tee /sys/bus/platform/devices/$NAME/driver_override
echo $NAME | sudo tee /sys/bus/platform/drivers/uio/bind
echo 1 | sudo tee /sys/bus/platform/devices/$NAME/uio/uio0/enable

Check:
ls /dev/uio*


4. DMA Sync Helper (required on Pi 5)
--------------------------------------
RP1 is a non-coherent DMA platform — buffers must be synced.

git clone https://github.com/rachel275/dma-sync-helper.git
cd dma-sync-helper
make

Load module:
sudo insmod dma_sync_helper.ko

Create device node (if needed):
sudo mknod /dev/dma_sync_eth0 c 240 0
sudo chmod 666 /dev/dma_sync_eth0

Export path (optional):
export MACB_DMA_SYNC_NODE=/dev/dma_sync_eth0


5. Run DPDK App (example)
--------------------------
sudo ./build/examples/dpdk-matrix-rx \
  --vdev=net_macb0,dev=/dev/uio0,phy_mode=auto \
  -l 0-1 -n 1 --no-pci


Quick Bring-Up Script
----------------------
sudo mkdir -p /dev/hugepages
sudo mount -t hugetlbfs nodev /dev/hugepages

sudo modprobe uio
NAME=$(ls /sys/bus/platform/devices | grep -i eth)
echo "uio" | sudo tee /sys/bus/platform/devices/$NAME/driver_override
echo $NAME | sudo tee /sys/bus/platform/drivers/uio/bind
echo 1 | sudo tee /sys/bus/platform/devices/$NAME/uio/uio0/enable

sudo insmod ~/dma-sync-helper/dma_sync_helper.ko
[ ! -e /dev/dma_sync_eth0 ] && sudo mknod /dev/dma_sync_eth0 c 240 0
sudo chmod 666 /dev/dma_sync_eth0


6. Run Matrix RX App
---------------------
cd app/matrix-rx
sudo ./matrix_rx \
  --vdev=net_macb0,dev=/dev/uio0,phy_mode=auto \
  -l 0-1 -n 1 --no-pci

7. Run testpmd (MACB, DMA-sync, Pi5)
-------------------------------------
sudo env MACB_RXTX_TRACE=2 ./build/app/dpdk-testpmd \
  -l 1-2 -n 1 --iova=pa \
  --huge-dir=/dev/hugepages --socket-mem=64 \
  --file-prefix=macb3 --no-telemetry --huge-unlink \
  --vdev='net_macb0,dev=/dev/uio0,phy_lb=0,mac_lb=0,phy_mode=100fd' \
  -- --interactive --nb-cores=1 --rxd=128 --txd=128 --total-num-mbufs=2048


Notes
------
- After UIO bind, eth0 disappears from Linux networking — expected
- Must repeat steps 2, 3, 4 after each reboot
- DMA sync helper strongly recommended for correctness and stability
